 "rulebook": "ITTF Handbook 2024, The Laws of Table Tennis: 2.1 (the table), 2.2 (the net assembly), 2.3 (the ball), 2.4 (the racket), 2.5 (definitions: struck, obstruction, racket hand, free hand), 2.6 (the service), 2.7 (the return), 2.10 (a point)",
 "deviations": [
  "2.6.1-2.6.3 (the service): there is no server and no toss: the ball waits at the server's strike point and is launched at 3.0 s with the position, velocity and spin it has at the strike of a legal service of RoboOlympics' serve library (a service a Franka arm made under its real limits; entry 221, the draw of this instance's seed, not chosen for being returnable), because the task is one return, not a match",
  "2.1.3 (the table's bounce): the playing surface is Google DeepMind's system-identified table, on which a ball dropped from 30 cm rebounds 26 cm, not the Law's about 23 cm; it is the only identified model of the ball's bounce with spin there is",
  "2.2 (the net): the net is a rigid wall 1 cm thick, 0.1525 m high, reaching 0.1525 m beyond each side line, without separate posts or a cord (DeepMind's model); a soft net is not simulated",
  "2.4 (the racket): the blade is a rigid 15 cm square, 1.5 cm thick, 0.14 kg with its handle (legal: the Laws set no size, shape or weight); its covering is one contact law on both faces (RoboOlympics' ball-paddle pair, hand-picked, not identified), and it is fixed to the right hand, because the robot's fist cannot hold a handle",
  "2.10.1.7 (deliberately striking the ball twice): intent cannot be judged: every contact of the blade or the racket hand within 0.05 s of the first is the same strike, a later one is a second strike",
  "2.10.1.8 (moving the playing surface): the table is fixed and this is not judged: a competition table weighs three times the robot and more, and the robot's push on it is limited by the grip of its own feet",
  "2.5.8 and 2.10.1 (the ball touching the player): any touch of the ball by anything but the blade or the racket hand loses the point, also after the ball has bounced on the robot's court (stricter than the Laws, which then only require that the return be struck)"
 ],
 "texts": {
  "sentence": "Return the service: after the table tennis ball has bounced once on your court, strike it with the paddle on your right hand so that it touches the opponent's court.",
  "rules": "The table is the official one: 2.74 m long, 1.525 m wide, its playing surface 0.76 m above the floor, with a net 0.1525 m high across its middle that reaches 0.1525 m beyond each side line. Your court is the half of the playing surface on your side of the net, the opponent's court the other half; the top edges belong to the playing surface, the sides of the table top do not (a ball that touches the table with its centre below the level of the playing surface has hit the side). The ball is the official one (40 mm, 2.7 g) and flies in air: drag and the lift of its spin act on it. A racket (a rigid square blade 0.15 m wide, 0.015 m thick, 0.14 kg with its handle) is fixed to your right hand, the racket hand; your left hand is the free hand. The service is struck 3.0 s after the start: a legal service, so the ball bounces first on the server's court, passes the net and bounces on your court. Then you must make a good return (Law 2.7): strike the ball, after that one bounce on your court, with the blade or with the racket hand below the wrist, so that it touches the opponent's court, directly or after touching the net; it may pass over or around the net. You succeed at the moment the ball touches the opponent's court after your strike. All contacts of the blade or the racket hand with the ball within 0.05 s of the first are one strike. You fail if you touch the ball before the service, or before it has bounced on your court (obstruction); if the ball bounces a second time on your court before you strike it (ball contacts with the table less than 0.02 s apart are one bounce); if anything of yours other than the blade or the racket hand touches the ball (the racket's handle, an arm, the body); if the blade or the racket hand touches the ball again later than 0.05 s after the first contact (struck twice, or carried); if after your strike the ball touches your own court, the side of the table or the floor before the opponent's court; if the ball touches the floor at any time; if any part of you or the racket touches the net; if your free hand touches the playing surface while the ball is in play (from the service on); if you fall; or after 8 s. Anything else of you, the racket included, may touch the table. A fall is your pelvis lower than 0.35 m above the floor, or any part of you other than your feet touching the floor (the racket on the floor is a fall).",
  "venue": "You stand at your end of the table facing it (+x), on its centre line, your pelvis 0.5 m behind your end line; the net is 1.37 m beyond the end line and the server's end line 2.74 m. The ball waits at rest where the server strikes it: 3.288919 m ahead of your pelvis, -0.199863 m to your left (negative: to your right) and 0.899714 m above the floor, just behind the server's end line. The service is one of a library of legal robot services: they leave the server's racket at 2.2 to 4.5 m/s with up to 34 rad/s of spin and bounce on your court between 0.2 and 0.8 s after the strike; short services bounce a second time on your court if you do not strike them. The paddle hangs from your right hand, its handle in the fist and the blade beyond it, the blade's centre 0.175 m from the wrist.",
  "difficulty": "The served ball is over your court for a few tenths of a second, low above a table that is as high as your hips, and may have to be met far inside the end line: the blade, a rigid extension of a 0.5 m arm, has to be there at the right instant and angle while the body leans over the table without touching the net. The return must clear a 15 cm net and still come down on a court 1.37 m long, off a ball that carries spin."
 },
